# Materials & Layer Separation

Source: https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/materials

Materials separate foreground (text, controls) from background (content) while letting color
pass through, so people keep their context. Keep two layers distinct: a **functional layer**
for controls and navigation that floats above a **content layer**.

## Layering rules

- Translucent "glass" treatment belongs to the functional layer **only**. In the content layer it makes controls compete with content and muddies hierarchy.
- **Never stack glass on glass.** For elements sitting on a translucent surface, use fills, transparency and vibrancy so they read as a thin overlay rather than a second pane.
- Never mix the `regular` and `clear` variants in the same context.
- Default to `regular`: it blurs and adjusts background luminosity to protect legibility. Required when the background could hurt legibility, or when the component holds significant text.
- Use `clear` only when **all three** hold: it sits over media-rich content, the content layer tolerates dimming, and the foreground content is bold and bright.
- With `clear` over bright content, add a dark dimming layer at **35% opacity**. Skip it when the underlying content is already dark.
- In resting states, avoid intersections between content and floating surfaces — reposition or scale the content to keep separation.

## Choosing thickness

- Thicker / more opaque → better contrast for text and fine-stroke elements.
- Thinner / more translucent → preserves context by keeping the background visible.

## Anti-patterns

- **Choosing a material for the color it appears to give the UI.** Choose by semantic meaning and recommended usage; system settings can change appearance.
- **Tinting everything.** Tint only primary elements and actions — if everything is tinted, nothing stands out. Brand color belongs in the content layer.
- **Solid fills on a translucent surface.** They break the character of the material; use the tinting system so the element stays grounded in its environment.

## Outside Apple platforms

Reduce Transparency, Increase Contrast and Reduce Motion are automatic on Apple platforms but
**not** in Qt, web or other toolkits. Implement the equivalents by hand: raise opacity and
frost the surface, use stark colors with contrasting borders, and disable elastic motion.
